Lab 3: Configure Automation Controller After Installation
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

• Configure authentication methods (LDAP or SSO) for the Automation Controller • Create and manage user roles with appropriate permissions • Integrate the Automation Controller with Private Automation Hub • Understand the security and access control mechanisms in Ansible Automation Platform • Implement best practices for user management and system integration

Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

• Basic understanding of Linux command line operations • Familiarity with Ansible concepts and terminology • Knowledge of LDAP authentication principles • Understanding of user roles and permissions concepts • Completed Lab 1 and Lab 2 (Automation Controller installation) • Access to a web browser for GUI configuration

Lab Environment
Ready-to-Use Cloud Machines: Al Nafi provides pre-configured Linux-based cloud machines for this lab. Simply click Start Lab to access your environment - no need to build your own VM or install software.

Your lab environment includes: • Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9 server with Automation Controller installed • Private Automation Hub instance • LDAP server for authentication testing • All necessary network configurations pre-configured

Task 1: Set Up Authentication (LDAP Integration)
Subtask 1.1: Access the Automation Controller Web Interface
Open your web browser and navigate to the Automation Controller URL:

https://your-controller-hostname
Log in using the default admin credentials created during installation:

Username: admin
Password: your-admin-password
Verify successful login by confirming you can see the Automation Controller dashboard.

Subtask 1.2: Configure LDAP Authentication Settings
Navigate to Settings by clicking on the gear icon in the top-right corner of the interface.

Select Authentication from the left sidebar menu.

Click on LDAP to configure LDAP authentication settings.

Configure the LDAP Server Connection by filling in the following fields:

{
  "AUTH_LDAP_SERVER_URI": "ldap://your-ldap-server:389",
  "AUTH_LDAP_BIND_DN": "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com",
  "AUTH_LDAP_BIND_PASSWORD": "your-ldap-password",
  "AUTH_LDAP_USER_SEARCH": [
    "ou=users,dc=example,dc=com",
    "SCOPE_SUBTREE",
    "(uid=%(user)s)"
  ]
}
Configure User Attribute Mapping:

{
  "AUTH_LDAP_USER_ATTR_MAP": {
    "first_name": "givenName",
    "last_name": "sn",
    "email": "mail"
  }
}
Set up Group Mapping for automatic role assignment:

{
  "AUTH_LDAP_GROUP_SEARCH": [
    "ou=groups,dc=example,dc=com",
    "SCOPE_SUBTREE",
    "(objectClass=groupOfNames)"
  ],
  "AUTH_LDAP_GROUP_TYPE": "GroupOfNamesType"
}
Subtask 1.3: Test LDAP Authentication
Save the LDAP configuration by clicking the Save button.

Test the connection by clicking Test next to the LDAP settings.

Verify successful connection by checking for a green checkmark or success message.

Create a test login by opening a new incognito/private browser window and attempting to log in with LDAP credentials.

Task 2: Create User Roles and Assign Permissions
