Task 3: Integrate with Private Automation Hub
Subtask 3.1: Configure Automation Hub Connection
Navigate to Settings and select Jobs from the left sidebar.

Scroll to the Automation Hub section and configure the following settings:

{
  "GALAXY_SERVER_LIST": [
    {
      "name": "Private Automation Hub",
      "url": "https://your-automation-hub-hostname/api/galaxy/",
      "auth_url": "https://your-automation-hub-hostname/api/galaxy/v3/auth/",
      "token": "your-automation-hub-token"
    }
  ]
}
Obtain the API token from your Private Automation Hub:

Log into your Automation Hub interface
Navigate to Collections → API Token
Copy the token value
Subtask 3.2: Configure Collection Downloads
Set the primary Galaxy server to your Private Automation Hub:

{
  "PRIMARY_GALAXY_URL": "https://your-automation-hub-hostname/api/galaxy/",
  "PRIMARY_GALAXY_TOKEN": "your-automation-hub-token",
  "PRIMARY_GALAXY_USERNAME": "",
  "PRIMARY_GALAXY_PASSWORD": ""
}
Configure collection path for automatic downloads:

{
  "COLLECTIONS_PATHS": [
    "/var/lib/awx/collections"
  ]
}
Save the configuration and test the connection.

Subtask 3.3: Test Integration with Collection Sync
Create a test project to verify the integration:

Navigate to Resources → Projects
Click Add to create a new project
Configure the project:

Name: Hub Integration Test
Organization: Default
SCM Type: Git
SCM URL: https://github.com/ansible/ansible-examples.git
Create a requirements file for collections:

# requirements.yml
collections:
  - name: community.general
    version: ">=1.0.0"
  - name: ansible.posix
    version: ">=1.0.0"
Test collection download by creating a job template that uses collections from your Private Automation Hub.

Subtask 3.4: Verify Hub Integration
Check collection availability by navigating to Collections in the Automation Controller.

Verify that collections are being pulled from your Private Automation Hub rather than the public Galaxy.

Test a playbook that uses collections to ensure they're properly accessible:

---
- name: Test Hub Integration
  hosts: localhost
  collections:
    - community.general
  tasks:
    - name: Test collection module
      community.general.timezone:
        name: UTC
Troubleshooting Tips
Common LDAP Issues
Problem: LDAP authentication fails Solution:

Verify LDAP server connectivity using ldapsearch command
Check bind DN and password accuracy
Ensure proper user search base configuration
Problem: Users can authenticate but have no permissions Solution:

Verify group mapping configuration
Check that LDAP groups exist and contain the expected users
Ensure role assignments are properly configured
User Management Issues
Problem: Custom roles not working as expected Solution:

Review permission assignments for the role
Check that users are properly assigned to roles
Verify organization-level permissions
Hub Integration Issues
Problem: Collections not downloading from Private Hub Solution:

Verify API token validity
Check network connectivity to Hub
Ensure proper SSL certificate configuration
Verification Steps
Test Authentication Configuration
Log out of the Automation Controller.

Attempt to log in using LDAP credentials.

Verify role-based access by logging in as different users and checking available options.

Test Hub Integration
Create a job template that requires collections.

Execute the job and verify collections are downloaded from your Private Hub.

Check the job output for collection source information.

Conclusion
In this lab, you have successfully:

• Configured LDAP authentication to integrate your Automation Controller with an external directory service, enabling centralized user management and single sign-on capabilities

• Created custom user roles with specific permissions, implementing the principle of least privilege and ensuring users have appropriate access levels for their responsibilities

• Established integration between Automation Controller and Private Automation Hub, creating a secure, controlled environment for collection management and distribution

• Implemented security best practices through proper user management, role-based access control, and secure authentication mechanisms

These configurations are essential for enterprise deployments of Ansible Automation Platform, as they provide the foundation for secure, scalable, and manageable automation environments. The authentication integration ensures that your automation platform works seamlessly with existing organizational infrastructure, while the Hub integration provides controlled access to certified and supported automation content.

Understanding these configuration aspects is crucial for the Red Hat Certified Specialist in Managing Automation with Ansible Automation Platform exam, as they represent core administrative tasks that automation platform administrators must master in production environments.
